      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C11"],
      "claim": "A benzimidazole-containing analogue reached 13 µM rat brain levels after intraperitoneal dosing, confirming that benzimidazole-piperidine-type structures can be brain penetrant when they lack the query's ionizable acid.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "low",
  "decision_summary": "The benzimidazole-piperidine-aryl scaffold is strongly supported as CNS-penetrant by multiple direct rat/PET brain-uptake records, but the query contains a free carboxylic acid plus a basic piperidine, forming a zwitterion that is expected to severely reduce passive BBB permeability. No experimental record directly covers an acid-bearing analogue, so the scaffold evidence is not sufficient to override this major physicochemical liability; meaningful CNS access is therefore considered unlikely.",
